  "dom_id": "step-13173",
  "name": "Example: \n                    Finding the Correct Quadrant for Negative Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation",
  "problem": "The angle $\\theta =  -435^\\circ$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-435^\\circ$ by *adding* integer multiples of $360^\\circ$ until we get a coterminal angle that lies in the range $[0^\\circ, 360^\\circ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{435}^{∘}+{360}^{∘} & =-{75}^{∘} \\\\ -{75}^{∘}+{360}^{∘} & ={285}^{∘}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the angle $285^\\circ$ is coterminal with $-435^\\circ.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14223930/assets/4f3fc79c2fc99478c407a63352d9113ef9cbef6a93a6feb62bd284d5f443bed5.png)\n\nWe see that $285^\\circ$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, $-435^\\circ$ also lies in this quadrant.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
